MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Operator directive: "generate a new energy model that is structurally the right
type" (a STATE-energy E(s), not the transition-energy). Cheap closure measurement:
does a contrastive state-energy discriminate win-states from non-win states
cross-game?

Result: leave-one-game-out (LOGO) AUROC = 0.496 ≈ chance; shuffled-label control
= 0.509 (both at chance => genuine null, not a harness artifact). 15 deepening
games, win-states from banked offline solves (1-6 per game, mostly 2).
state_energy_discriminates = false. adversarial_verify clean.

Interpretation: the structurally-correct state-energy does NOT even work as a
cross-game discriminator -- win-states are too few + too heterogeneous across
games to learn a transferable energy. So beyond the selection-vs-generation role
mismatch, the MODEL itself can't be learned from the available win-states. Caveat:
CROSS-GAME (LOGO); a per-game energy is untested here (too few wins/game) and the
existing offline LearnedVerifier already fills the per-game state-scorer slot.

Closes the energy-of-the-right-type line empirically. Cross-refs the
offline-codex verdict (state-energy can't help offline solves: loop already has a
state-energy heuristic; wall is generation; dev proxy).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
